event: response.function_call_arguments.done
data: {"type":"response.function_call_arguments.done","sequence_number":3200,"item_id":"fc_03d475638b3efdb5016adf27b08b10819182483340a2e3882d","output_index":3,"arguments":"{\"artifact\":{\"base_candidate\":1,\"patches\":[{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/steps\",\"value\":[{\"id\":\"T001\",\"statement\":\"LR.Strings lam x y h v\",\"inputs\":[\"H003\",\"H004\",\"H005\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S001\"],\"explanation\":\"Induct on the nonnegative lowering exponent using the three commutators; the successor version of the raising formula includes the m=0 case.\"},{\"id\":\"T002\",\"statement\":\"(∀ i : LR.Idx lam.toNat, LR.e y v i.val ≠ 0) ∧ LinearIndependent ℂ (fun i : LR.Idx lam.toNat => LR.e y v i.val)\",\"inputs\":[\"H003\",\"H005\",\"T001\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S002\"],\"explanation\":\"Iterate raising to show nonvanishing. Isolate each coefficient using the product of h minus the other complex weights; all differences are nonzero in characteristic zero.\"},{\"id\":\"T003\",\"statement\":\"(∃ B : Module.Basis (LR.Idx lam.toNat) ℂ V, LR.IsStringBasis lam.toNat y v B) ∧ (∀ m : ℕ, lam.toNat < m → (y^m) v = 0)\",\"inputs\":[\"H003\",\"H004\",\"T001\",\"T002\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S003\"],\"explanation\":\"Use dimension to upgrade independence to a basis. The next lowering vector has an absent eigenvalue, hence is zero in that basis; further powers vanish.\"},{\"id\":\"T004\",\"statement\":\"LR.StringResult lam x y h v\",\"inputs\":[\"H003\",\"T001\",\"T002\",\"T003\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S004\"],\"explanation\":\"Derive the basic x,y,h actions, then iterate and divide by factorials to obtain both integral binomial formulas, including the zero branches. Package N001.\"},{\"id\":\"T005\",\"statement\":\"∀ q : ℚ, q ∈ LR.R p ↔ ∃ a b : ℤ, b ≠ 0 ∧ ¬ (p : ℤ) ∣ b ∧ q = (a : ℚ)/(b : ℚ)\",\"inputs\":[\"H001\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S005\"],\"explanation\":\"Prove that fractions with p-prime denominators form a subring, so the closure definition of R has exactly the intended carrier. This is a checked representation bridge.\"},{\"id\":\"T006\",\"statement\":\"Nonempty (LR.LatticeEmbedding p lam.toNat M)\",\"inputs\":[\"H001\",\"H004\",\"H006\",\"T004\",\"T005\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S005\",\"S006\"],\"explanation\":\"The H006 basis is reindexed by Fin(l+1), equivalently the source reindexing by dimension. Base-change it to R. Complex independence proves injectivity of tensor multiplication and identifies its range with the R-span MR; obtain the displayed equivalence and complex spanning.\"},{\"id\":\"T007\",\"statement\":\"LR.Preserves p lam.toNat M x y h\",\"inputs\":[\"H001\",\"H003\",\"H004\",\"H006\",\"T004\",\"T005\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S007\"],\"explanation\":\"Restrict the divided powers to M and extend preservation to its R-span. Restrict h=[x,y]. Prime-to-p factorials and the binomial-factorial identity give all required units in R.\"},{\"id\":\"T008\",\"statement\":\"∀ B : Module.Basis (LR.Idx lam.toNat) ℂ V, LR.IsStringBasis lam.toNat y v B → LR.LocalResult p lam.toNat M x y h B\",\"inputs\":[\"T004\",\"T005\",\"T006\",\"T007\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S008\"],\"explanation\":\"Compute the three matrix-unit identities on the characteristic-zero basis. Invert the binomial unit in R and compose to show every coordinate matrix unit preserves MR. Package N002, including the proved tensor embedding and integral restrictions.\"},{\"id\":\"T009\",\"statement\":\"∀ B : Module.Basis (LR.Idx lam.toNat) ℂ V, LR.IsStringBasis lam.toNat y v B → Nonempty (LR.Decomposition p lam.toNat M v B)\",\"inputs\":[\"H001\",\"T004\",\"T008\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S009\"],\"explanation\":\"Prove the exact membership characterizations of the span-defined Jc objects. Matrix units identify all coordinate modules with J at e0=v. Projection of a finite basis gives finite generation; spanning gives nonzeroness. The coordinate sum defines the R-linear equivalence.\"},{\"id\":\"T010\",\"statement\":\"∀ B : Module.Basis (LR.Idx lam.toNat) ℂ V, LR.IsStringBasis lam.toNat y v B → LR.RationalEmbedding p M v\",\"inputs\":[\"H001\",\"T009\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S010\"],\"explanation\":\"Construct multiplication Q tensor_R J to C by balancing. Put finitely many rational tensor factors over one nonzero integer denominator to prove injectivity. Base-change a finite generating family to prove finite dimensionality.\"},{\"id\":\"T011\",\"statement\":\"∀ B : Module.Basis (LR.Idx lam.toNat) ℂ V, LR.IsStringBasis lam.toNat y v B → ∀ d : LR.Decomposition p lam.toNat M v B, ∃ Phi : (ℚ ⊗[LR.R p] LR.MR p M) ≃ₗ[ℚ] (LR.Idx lam.toNat → LR.T p M v), ∀ q c, Phi (q ⊗ₜ[LR.R p] d.coordEquiv c) = fun i => q ⊗ₜ[LR.R p] c i\",\"inputs\":[\"H001\",\"T009\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S011\"],\"explanation\":\"Rationalize the coordinate isomorphism. Define both maps by balanced tensor maps, with inverse the finite sum of the coordinate injections, and check their composites on elementary tensors.\"},{\"id\":\"T012\",\"statement\":\"∀ B : Module.Basis (LR.Idx lam.toNat) ℂ V, LR.IsStringBasis lam.toNat y v B → Module.finrank ℚ (LR.T p M v) = 1\",\"inputs\":[\"H001\",\"T004\",\"T008\",\"T009\",\"T010\",\"T011\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S012\"],\"explanation\":\"The rationalized localized lattice has dimension n from its R-basis. The checked coordinate isomorphism gives dimension n*r on the other side. Finite dimensionality and n>0 imply r=1.\"},{\"id\":\"T013\",\"statement\":\"∀ B : Module.Basis (LR.Idx lam.toNat) ℂ V, LR.IsStringBasis lam.toNat y v B → LR.CoordinateResult p lam.toNat M v B\",\"inputs\":[\"H001\",\"T004\",\"T009\",\"T010\",\"T012\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S013\"],\"explanation\":\"A nonzero a in J gives a nonzero rational tensor. Rank one makes it a rational basis; multiplication then expresses each c in J as a rational multiple of a. Package all of N003.\"},{\"id\":\"T014\",\"statement\":\"∀ B : Module.Basis (LR.Idx lam.toNat) ℂ V, LR.IsStringBasis lam.toNat y v B → ∃ k : ℕ, 0 < k ∧ ∃ g : Fin k → ℂ, (∀ j, g j ∈ LR.Jc p M v ∧ g j ≠ 0) ∧ LR.Jc p M v = Submodule.span (LR.R p) (Set.range g)\",\"inputs\":[\"H001\",\"T013\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S014\"],\"explanation\":\"Choose finite generators of the nonzero module J and delete zero entries. Reindex the remaining nonempty finite list.\"},{\"id\":\"T015\",\"statement\":\"∀ B : Module.Basis (LR.Idx lam.toNat) ℂ V, LR.IsStringBasis lam.toNat y v B → ∀ (k : ℕ) (g : Fin k → ℂ), 0 < k → (∀ j, g j ∈ LR.Jc p M v ∧ g j ≠ 0) → ∃ a : ℂ, a ∈ LR.Jc p M v ∧ a ≠ 0 ∧ ∃ nu : Fin k → ℤ, ∃ u : Fin k → (LR.R p)ˣ, (∀ j, g j = ((((p : ℚ) ^ nu j) * ((u j : LR.R p) : ℚ) : ℚ) : ℂ) * a) ∧ ∃ j0 : Fin k, ∀ j, nu j0 ≤ nu j\",\"inputs\":[\"H001\",\"T013\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S015\"],\"explanation\":\"Use the rational-line assertion, factor each nonzero rational coefficient as p to an integer power times a unit of R, and choose the minimum exponent. The valuation/unit factorization may use the rational p-adic valuation library directly.\"},{\"id\":\"T016\",\"statement\":\"∀ B : Module.Basis (LR.Idx lam.toNat) ℂ V, LR.IsStringBasis lam.toNat y v B → LR.PrincipalResult p lam.toNat M v B\",\"inputs\":[\"H001\",\"T013\",\"T014\",\"T015\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S016\"],\"explanation\":\"The generator with minimum exponent gives b and J=Rb. The coordinate decomposition proves that b times every e_i is a basis. Repeat for any nonzero principal generator, as required by N004.\"},{\"id\":\"T017\",\"statement\":\"Nonempty (LR.FirstChange p K M)\",\"inputs\":[\"H001\",\"H002\",\"T005\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S017\"],\"explanation\":\"Construct rho by inverting p-prime denominators, and the natural scalar-first tensor associativity equivalence. Check its inverse and elementary tensor formula. The scalar-first convention is the canonical flip of the source convention.\"},{\"id\":\"T018\",\"statement\":\"Nonempty (LR.ScalarData p K M)\",\"inputs\":[\"H001\",\"H002\",\"T005\",\"T017\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S018\"],\"explanation\":\"Prove R/pR is ZMod p with its fraction formula. Give MR/pMR its canonical ZMod p module structure. Construct the second natural tensor equivalence by taking classes, with inverse independent of the lift because p acts as zero on K.\"},{\"id\":\"T019\",\"statement\":\"∀ B : Module.Basis (LR.Idx lam.toNat) ℂ V, LR.IsStringBasis lam.toNat y v B → Nonempty (LR.ReducedBasis p K lam.toNat M v B)\",\"inputs\":[\"H001\",\"H002\",\"T004\",\"T008\",\"T016\",\"T018\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S019\"],\"explanation\":\"Choose a principal generator and its localized basis. Base-change along rho and transport back along A to beta. Coordinate reduction gives the ZMod p quotient basis. Compute the dimension and positive cardinality.\"},{\"id\":\"T020\",\"statement\":\"∀ B : Module.Basis (LR.Idx lam.toNat) ℂ V, LR.IsStringBasis lam.toNat y v B → ∀ rb : LR.ReducedBasis p K lam.toNat M v B, ∀ o : LR.IntegralOps M x y h, LR.ReducedAction K lam.toNat M x y h o rb.beta\",\"inputs\":[\"H001\",\"H002\",\"T004\",\"T008\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S020\"],\"explanation\":\"The natural scalar maps intertwine restrictions and tensor extensions, checked on elementary tensors. Transfer the characteristic-zero formulas to b e_i and then beta. Tensor extension preserves brackets and the integer identities r! X_r=x^r and r! Y_r=y^r; the factorial images are nonzero.\"},{\"id\":\"T021\",\"statement\":\"∀ B : Module.Basis (LR.Idx lam.toNat) ℂ V, LR.IsStringBasis lam.toNat y v B → ∀ rb : LR.ReducedBasis p K lam.toNat M v B, ∀ o : LR.IntegralOps M x y h, LR.ReducedControl K lam.toNat M x y h o rb.beta\",\"inputs\":[\"H001\",\"T004\",\"T008\",\"T020\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S021\"],\"explanation\":\"Transfer the matrix-unit product identity to beta (or verify it there using the transferred formulas). A binomial unit stays nonzero. An x,y-stable K-subspace is stable under powers, scalar multiples, and all products appearing in that identity.\"},{\"id\":\"T022\",\"statement\":\"∃ B : Module.Basis (LR.Idx lam.toNat) ℂ V, LR.IsStringBasis lam.toNat y v B ∧ LR.ReductionResult p K lam.toNat M x y h v B\",\"inputs\":[\"T004\",\"T008\",\"T019\",\"T020\",\"T021\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S021\"],\"explanation\":\"Choose the string basis and package the scalar identifications, quotient and reduced bases, tensor-induced operators, all reduced formulas, and matrix-unit invariance. This realizes the complete N005 output.\"},{\"id\":\"T023\",\"statement\":\"∀ beta : Module.Basis (LR.Idx lam.toNat) K (LR.VK K M), Nontrivial (LR.VK K M) ∧ ∀ W : Submodule K (LR.VK K M), W ≠ ⊥ → ∃ w : LR.VK K M, w ∈ W ∧ w ≠ 0 ∧ ∃ j : LR.Idx lam.toNat, beta.repr w j ≠ 0\",\"inputs\":[],\"source_steps\":[\"S022\"],\"explanation\":\"Any such nonempty basis makes the space nonzero. In a nonzero subspace choose w≠0; injectivity of basis coordinates gives a nonzero coordinate.\"},{\"id\":\"T024\",\"statement\":\"∀ beta : Module.Basis (LR.Idx lam.toNat) K (LR.VK K M), ∀ W : Submodule K (LR.VK K M), (∀ i j, LR.Inv W (LR.MU beta i j)) → W = ⊥ ∨ W = ⊤\",\"inputs\":[\"T023\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S023\"],\"explanation\":\"In the nonzero branch, apply each coordinate matrix unit to w. Divide by its nonzero j-th coordinate to put every basis vector in W, so W is top.\"},{\"id\":\"T025\",\"statement\":\"Nontrivial (LR.VK K M) ∧ ∃ o : LR.IntegralOps M x y h, ∀ W : Submodule K (LR.VK K M), LR.Inv W (o.xz.baseChange K) → LR.Inv W (o.yz.baseChange K) → LR.Inv W (o.hz.baseChange K) → W = ⊥ ∨ W = ⊤\",\"inputs\":[\"H001\",\"H003\",\"H004\",\"T022\",\"T023\",\"T024\"],\"source_steps\":[\"S022\",\"S024\"],\"explanation\":\"Use the N005 reduced basis for nonzeroness, and its matrix-unit invariance with the independent matrix-unit subspace lemma. Universally quantify over W. The generators span L(K), including characteristic 2; the singleton l=0 case requires no special argument. This is P.\"}]},{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/terminal\",\"value\":\"T025\"},{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/source_outputs\",\"value\":{\"N001\":\"T004\",\"N002\":\"T008\",\"N003\":\"T013\",\"N004\":\"T016\",\"N005\":\"T022\",\"P\":\"T025\"}}]}}"}

